Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:03.980 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  last weekend I was riding in an uber  express pool my fellow female passenger  was angry she was dropped so far from  her destination so she tried to punch  the driver and steal his phone the  driver got out of the car pushing her  away defending himself  cops sped up pulling the two apart  assuming it was the driver's fault as he  was being interrogated by the police he  kept begging for his phone I need to  turn off my uber app he pled if I don't  pick up my next client they will charge  me for the trip and deactivate me from  the platform this 60-something Korean  man was more afraid of the wrath of uber  than the wrath of the police and rightly  so  uber could have unilaterally cut off his  livelihood with no hearing no notice and  no appeal this driver is an independent  contractor which in theory means he  should have complete independence over  setting his own rates working conditions  and contract terms however gate  platforms like uber use changing  incentives and the threat of  deactivation to exert unprecedented  control over their allegedly independent  workforce these statements may lead you  to believe that I'm anti business but  actually I worked it over when I joined  the company running their East African  operations  I was excited about creating thousands  of economic opportunities for people in  Kenya Tanzania and Uganda where  unemployment rates are really high and I  was excited about improving mobility and  today's hungry for convenient affordable  transportation but that excitement  quickly gave way to disillusionment  every two weeks I hosted focus groups  with around 20 drivers they told me  Alisa we're making no money on this  platform we work for a hundred hours  every week and they only take home $20  when I ran the historical data their  stories checked out  when uber first launched in the East  Africa they offered very generous rates  and bonuses to make the economic  opportunity and no-brainer drivers with  the encouragement of uber took out  multi-year loans to finance the  relatively new vehicles required to join  the platform one year after launch and  two weeks after I joined the company we  slashed rates by 35% shifting all of  that onto drivers it would not be the  last bait-and-switch the company would  pull when I raised concerns about driver  earnings to management their message to  me was clear gig workers are an  expendable resource for growth because  of the company's labor practices I quit  after a short six months - ironically  become an independent worker myself as  an independent worker I faced many  challenges like client's ghosting when  it came time to make their final payment  and going 18 months without health  insurance in Kenya 80% of adults are  self-employed this means that you have  no guaranteed salary no retirement  contributions no paid leave no employer  sponsored benefits and you have to pay  all your own expenses the issue of the  lack of protections for contract workers  may seem like a distant emerging-market  problem but the US economy is evolving  to look like Kenya's since 2010 the IRS  has reported a six percent year-on-year  increase in the number of independent  relative to full-time workers by 2020  half of Americans will be self employed  full-time w-2 workers in this country  have several protections thanks to the  labor movement which started around the  time of the Industrial Revolution  factory owners treated their workers as  expendable forcing them to work a  hundred hour weeks just to maximize  profits workers organized into unions  and rioted resulting in the 1938 Fair  Labor Standards Act which instituted the  40-hour work week ban child labor and  set standards around minimum wage and  overtime  pay none of these protections apply to  contractors which threatens to increase  the unprecedented levels of inequality  we see in this country corporations are  systemically contorting full-time  positions into contract positions to  minimize their fixed labor costs  oftentimes contractors will work for the  same single company for years by  changing nothing more than that  individuals formal employment status  companies are sidestepping the hard one  worker protections of the 20th century  and shifting risk from investors to  workers why do I care so much about this  Mis classification issue well my dad was  classified as an independent contractor  for the 25 years he worked at his  company he made less than $20,000 and  received no benefits if my mom a public  schoolteacher did not have generous  benefits I have no idea how he would  have retired or my family would have  access quality health insurance  some of the worst offenders of contract  worker abuse are in our own backyard  I learned five minutes before this  session started that Stanford is  increasingly shifting to contractors  rather than full-time employees to  reduce their benefits obligations such  as giving their employees free college  tuition for their children the  communications coaches who helped us  with these presentations are in short  term rather than full-time contracts  Google has more contractors on its  payroll than full-time employees how  many of you have ordered something on  Amazon Prime in the past month all of us  say Wow chances are that that package  was delivered to you by an Amazon flex  contract employee who post expenses can  make as little as five dollars an hour  under half of California's eleven dollar  minimum wage  contract workers in Arizona moderate  explicit content on the Facebook  platform they spend hours every day  watching horrific videos of murder and  sexual exploitation several suffer from  PTSD which Facebook of course provides  no support for because they're just  contractors not employees these  contractors make $15 an hour or a mere  $28,000 a year Silicon Valley fed by  Stanford talent has profited from  driving down the wages of low-skilled  workers we moved backwards as a society  back to where we were at the start of  the industrial revolution back to the  age of the hundred hour workweek and no  effective minimum wage back to the age  where workers rights are dependent on  the benevolence of management dependent  on the whims of the people in this room  rather than protected by law the good  news is that gig workers are organizing  to demand their rights in the past few  weeks  instacart shoppers took to Reddit and  Facebook forums to oppose instacart s--  policy of deducting tips from their  minimum guaranteed base pay this time  the workers won instacart  agreed to change its policy and pay out  tips separately from base pay and they  instituted new minimum payments per  delivery David won against Goliath I'd  like to leave you with this by being at  the GSB we are in a place of incredible  privilege our starting salary coming out  of this school will be a hundred and ten  thousand dollars higher than or two  point eight X the median household  income in the United States your  employer will almost certainly give you  health insurance and paid leave and  retirement contribution on top of  dinners at Nobu free trips to Cancun and  souls like al qasas I say that's not to  kill to you but to implore you to be a  voice for those who do not have  the same level of privilege you may be  wondering what you can do well you can  start by tipping tip generously and tip  with cash or another form of payment  where you know the funds will directly  reach workers beyond tipping you can  email HR if you witness contract worker  mistreatment within your organization if  you work in big tech chances are that  mistreatment exists if you see contract  workers working crazy hours or earning  wages that post expenses are below  minimum wage or see workers doing the  work of a full-time employee so they  deserve full benefits and if your  management is unwilling to listen like  mine was you can have the courage to go  to the press even anonymously  publications like the New York Times  recode and Bloomberg have exposed  stories that have shamed companies like  Facebook and instant ooh making changes  the disappearance of quality employment  opportunities threatens to exacerbate  the unprecedented levels of inequality  that we see in our country and our Bay  Area community searching your ethical  responsibilities to work workers may  seem like smart business but it stuns  the economic mobility of your workers  and their children don't be a part of  the problem be a part of the solution  towards just employment thank you  [Applause]  [Music]  you

